WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.190
The 208 Billions dollar a year Hotel Industry
is massively competitive.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:10.299
Location, branding and architecture are all
very important.

00:00:10.299 --> 00:00:15.769
But in here is where the competition gets
particularly heated.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:19.840
Hotel Amenities, which are pretty much everything
that’s provided by hotels outside of the

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:26.349
room and bed are an easy way for one hotel
to set themselves apart from others.

00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:31.589
Some amenities we’ve come to take for granted,
TV seems more like a minimal requirement than

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:34.030
an added feature by this point.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:38.239
Some amenities seem obvious but have never
caught on.

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:42.539
Why are three different soaps and two kinds
of lotion commonly available but toothpaste

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:43.539
never provided?

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:47.629
Occasionally a chain of hotels introduces
an innovative amenity, and the other chain’s

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.879
race to catch up and one-up each other creates
a intense period of amenity competition.

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:58.670
Let’s look at these hotel amenities wars,
and see if we maybe we’re in the midst of

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:06.670
another one right now.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:11.200
The most recent and intense Amenity war centered
around bedding.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.980
It started in 1999 when Starwood’s Westin
introduced the “Heavenly Bed” the floral

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.040
prints bedspreads were replace with a white-on-white
bedding package by Simmons with a pillow top

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.790
mattress containing 900 coils, three sheets,
(ranging in thread count from 180 to 250)

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.410
and a down blanket available in three different
thicknesses depending on the climate.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:34.600
The move to white-on-white bedding actually
caused the cleanliness scores for the hotel

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.680
to go up.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.340
Not only did things look cleaner, but the
hotels were actually forced to watch the bedding

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.270
more frequently since the white bedding didn’t
hide dirt and stains as well as the darker

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.549
floral patterns.

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:52.000
Hyatt was not to be left behind.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.130
They fired back with the “Grand Bed,”
a Sealy Posturepedic mattress with down blankets

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.539
and 250-thread-count triple sheeting.

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:01.930
And none of this was cheap.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:08.610
Marriott spent $190 million to upgrade beds
at its family of hotels, each king-size bed

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:14.720
at Marriott featured 300-thread-count cotton
sheets, seven pillows, a fluffy mattress cover

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.450
and a white duvet.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:22.950
Each chain introduced their own take on luxurious
bedding, the battle culminating with The Pan

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:31.050
Pacific Vancouver installing $10,000 British-made
Hypnos Duchess mattress.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:35.300
But the war wouldn’t end there, it would
move to the bathroom before settling down

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:46.080
for a while, Westin spending over $10 millions
dollars to upgrade it’s bathroom amenities.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.740
When an amenity shows up in an amenity war,
it doesn’t always stick around.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:57.640
The the 60s the battles were fought over color
televisions, remote controls and clock radios.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.320
Those are here to stay.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:04.880
But the sewing kits, and shoe horns of the
70’s are relics of the era.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.880
Even the mini-bar rising to prominence in
the 90s is being phased out for the most part.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.990
Hotels choose amenities that they think will
provide a feeling of luxury at a low cost

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:22.560
to the hotel, so they don’t necessarily
go for the stuff you expect to have at home.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.870
Sometimes in the race to set themselves apart
hotels introduce amenities that border on

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.160
the absurd.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:35.660
Wax for sealing letters, cowboy hats and bandanas,
cats that can visit your room, video cameras,

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:40.880
dictionaries, in-room exercise bikes, and
rare books have all made an appearance as

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.970
amenities over the years.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:48.640
While these things might seem like nice free
bonuses for guests, it’s important to remember

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.230
that you’re paying for the cost of the amenities
somewhere.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:59.360
The $20 worth of soaps and coffee might be
subsidized by the $30 a night parking fee.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:03.550
And they can generate a massive amount of
waste.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:09.880
Hotels and travelers combined throw out as
many as 5 million used bars of soap a day.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.590
Charity organizations like Clean the World,
are working to recycle as much of this as

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:18.560
possible, providing soap to places that need
it.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.820
But currently only 20% of US hotels are involved,
meaning the large majority of soap is still

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.020
being thrown away.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:30.810
So when can we expect the next amenities war?

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.620
We might be on the brink of one at the moment.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:40.080
This year a localized skirmish in Nashville
erupted, leading to chef-prepared meals for

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:47.290
pets, recording studios, and a choice of buckwheat,
latex or goose-down pillows are all being

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:51.650
offered as amenities at various hotels in
the area.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.730
Maybe the most interesting amenity I’ve
come across?

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:55.730
Humans.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:05:01.180
In 2016 Westin started it’s running concierge
program in an effort to bring locals around

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:07.440
the hotel in an effort to provide a more local
atmosphere for the guests who like to run.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:13.110
Ultimately hotel amenities are a great case
study for how competition in a market can

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:18.780
generate both useful progress that benefits
the customer, and ridiculous features that

00:05:18.780 --> 00:06:20.410
will be laughed at

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:26.000
around campfires for generations.

